<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zipoetes I of Bithynia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 278 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zipoetes I was a ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a> in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> who successfully resisted the Hellenistic kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I</a>. Ruling from about 326 BCE as a local dynast, he took the title of king in 297 BCE, founding the Bithynian royal dynasty and the era by which Bithynia later counted its years. He was succeeded by his son Nicomedes I.</p></div>
